The circuit is an <strong>inverting Schmitt trigger</strong>. The op-amp output swing is \[V_{\text{sat}}=\pm\frac{30}{2}=\pm15\,\text{V}.\] The switching thresholds are \[V_{UT}=\frac{R_{2}}{R_{1}+R_{2}}V_{\text{sat}}
=\frac{5}{10+5}\times15
=5\,\text{V},\] \[V_{LT}=\frac{R_{2}}{R_{1}+R_{2}}(-V_{\text{sat}})
=-5\,\text{V}.\] The saw-tooth waveform varies from \(-10\,\text{V}\) to \(+10\,\text{V}\), hence \[V_{i}=\frac{20}{T}t-10.\] <img src="img/d0fb46a90177fb86.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,224px)"> At the switching instant, \[5=\frac{20}{T}t-10\] \[t=\frac{3T}{4}.\] Thus, <ul><li>\(V_{o}=+15\,\text{V}\) for \(\dfrac{3T}{4}\),</li><li>\(V_{o}=-15\,\text{V}\) for \(\dfrac{T}{4}\).</li></ul> Hence, \[V_{o(\text{avg})}
=\frac{15\left(\frac{3T}{4}\right)-15\left(\frac{T}{4}\right)}{T}
=7.5\,\text{V}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(d) }7.5\,\text{V}}\]

The circuit is a <strong>non-inverting amplifier</strong> with <img src="img/444ae15d20f70069.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> \[R_{1}=1\,\text{k}\Omega,\qquad
R_{f}=R_{L}.\] Hence, the output voltage is \[V_{o}
=\left(1+\frac{R_{L}}{1\,\text{k}\Omega}\right)V_{in}.\] Given, \[V_{in}(t)=2\sin(100\pi t)\,\text{V},\] therefore, \[V_{o}
=\left(1+\frac{R_{L}}{1\,\text{k}\Omega}\right)
2\sin(100\pi t).\] The current through \(R_{L}\) is \[I_{R_{L}}
=\frac{V_{o}}{R_{L}+1\,\text{k}\Omega}.\] Substituting \(V_{o}\), \[I_{R_{L}}
=
\frac{\left(1+\dfrac{R_{L}}{1\,\text{k}\Omega}\right)
2\sin(100\pi t)}
{R_{L}+1\,\text{k}\Omega}.\] Since, \[1+\frac{R_{L}}{1\,\text{k}\Omega}
=\frac{R_{L}+1\,\text{k}\Omega}{1\,\text{k}\Omega},\] we get \[I_{R_{L}}
=
\frac{2\sin(100\pi t)}
{1\,\text{k}\Omega}
=2\sin(100\pi t)\,\text{mA}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) }+2\sin(100\pi t)\,\text{mA}}\]

The Schmitt trigger has a hysteresis band of \[\pm0.1\,\text{V}.\] Hence, the output changes state when the input reaches \[V_{i}=0.1\,\text{V}.\] The input is \[V_{i}=5\sin(1000\pi t).\] Therefore, \[5\sin(1000\pi t)=0.1\] \[\sin(1000\pi t)=0.02.\] Since \(0.02\ll1\), \[1000\pi t\approx0.02\ \text{rad}.\] Thus, \[t=\frac{0.02}{1000\pi}
=6.37\times10^{-6}\,\text{s}
=6.37\,\mu\text{s}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) }6.37\,\mu\text{s}}\]

From the given Routh-Hurwitz array: \[\begin{array}{c|cc}
    s^3 &amp; 1 &amp; b \\
    s^2 &amp; a &amp; c \\
    s^1 &amp; \dfrac{6-k}{3} &amp; 0 \\
    s^0 &amp; k &amp; 
\end{array}\] For critical stability, the row of \(s^1\) must be zero: \[\frac{6-k}{3} = 0 \implies k = 6\] The auxiliary equation is formed from the \(s^2\) row: \[A(s) = a s^2 + c = 0\] Given \(a = 3\): \[3s^2 + c = 0 \implies s = \pm j\sqrt{\dfrac{c}{3}}\] From the root locus diagram, the system crosses the imaginary axis at \(j\omega = \pm j1.414 = \pm j\sqrt{2}\). Comparing the roots: \[\sqrt{\frac{c}{3}} = \sqrt{2} \implies c = 6\] \[\boxed{\text{(A) 6 and 6}}\]

The distance from the roll gap where the thickness is measured is, \(L=0.5\,\text{m},\) and the speed of the sheet is \(v=0.2\,\text{m/s}.\) Therefore, the time taken by the sheet to travel this distance is: \[T=\frac{L}{v}
=\frac{0.5}{0.2}
=2.5\,\text{s}\] Hence, the measured thickness is delayed by \(2.5\,\text{s}\) with respect to the roll gap, i.e., \[m(t)=d(t-2.5)\] \[M(s)=e^{-2.5s}D(s)\] Therefore, \[G(s)=\frac{M(s)}{D(s)}
=e^{-2.5s}\] \[\boxed{(A)\;e^{-2.5s}}\]
